• Now I have the in-progress faithful detector (`detectTracesInteger_` rewritten). Key observations so far for question 1 (faithfulness differences vs OpenMS MassTraceDetection.cpp):

  1. **`used` marking timing**: OpenMS marks peaks visited ONLY for traces that pass `isTraceValid_` (MassTraceDetection.cpp:656-660) — invalid traces leave their peaks free to be re-seeded. The integer detector marks `used[k]=1` at extension time (line 829) and never unmarks on rejection (lines 841, 851-852 `continue` after marking). This changes which traces exist, not just order: rejected traces consume their peaks in the integer version, so those peaks can't seed or join other traces. Massive behavioral difference. The design's section 3 sketch says "used[k] = true" at take time — same flaw. This is the biggest faithfulness break.

  2. **Termination criterion**: OpenMS default `trace_termination_criterion` is "outlier" (5 consecutive misses, MassTraceDetection.cpp:41-43). The tool doesn't set it — check `detectTraces_` lines 1357-1373: it sets mass_error_ppm, noise, snr, ion_mobility_tolerance, min_sample_rate conditionally — need to check if it sets trace_termination_criterion. Grep showed no setValue("trace_termination_criterion"...) in DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp. So the current OpenMS path uses OUTLIER mode: stop a direction after 5 CONSECUTIVE misses. The design reimplements SAMPLE_RATE mode (hits/visited < min_sample_rate). These are different stopping rules! In OpenMS, min_sample_rate in OUTLIER mode is only used at `isTraceValid_` as a quality check (trace.size()/adjusted_scans), not for termination. The sketch uses it for termination. So the sketch's claim "stop a direction when the hit rate over visited frames falls below min_sample_rate" describes OpenMS's sample_rate mode, which is NOT the mode the tool runs. Also note OpenMS sample_rate mode has `min_scans_to_consider = 5` grace and uses COMBINED hits/scans across both directions (down_state.hitting + up_state.hitting), whereas the sketch computes per-direction hits/visited. Both differ from the sketch.

  Also isTraceValid_: quality = trace.size() / (total_scans - consecutive_missed_down - consecutive_missed_up) — adjusted scans subtract the trailing misses. The integer detector has no equivalent final quality check at all — it only checks length. Wait: with min_sample_rate termination per direction, trace quality is implicitly ≥ min_sample_rate? Not quite — combined hits over combined visited. If a trace goes 5 hits then 5 misses in each direction... per-direction rate can be ≥ threshold while total rate lower? Actually per-direction rate ≥ msr at break time for each direction, but the final trace's rate = hits/(visited) over both directions, each ≥ msr individually... hmm, at the point of break, hits/visited < msr triggered the break, so final per-direction rate is below msr (the break happens exactly when it dips below). So final overall rate < msr. OpenMS isTraceValid_ would then REJECT the trace if quality < min_sample_rate — but OpenMS subtracts trailing consecutive misses from denominator, which rescues it. In outlier mode, trace ends after 5 consecutive misses, so adjusted_scans excludes those 5 per direction. Net: OpenMS outlier-mode traces must satisfy quality ≥ 0.5 after subtracting trailing misses. The integer sketch has NO final validity check by sample rate at all — traces emitted regardless of final hit rate. That changes which traces exist (more, lower-quality traces emitted). And the peaks of those traces are consumed, preventing re-seeding.

  3. **Peak membership threshold**: OpenMS removes peaks ≤ noise_threshold_int_ from work_exp entirely (line 243: `tmp_peak_int > noise_threshold_int_` strictly greater); the integer version filters seed order by `inten > noise` (line 767) but `pick()` (line 788-795) does NOT check intensity at all — any peak in the frame can be taken as a trace member even if below noise. In OpenMS, below-noise peaks don't exist in work_exp, so they can't be extension candidates. This changes trace membership: the integer detector will append noise-level peaks to traces, shifting centroids and affecting sample rates. Also note in OpenMS the apex check is `>` snr*noise; integer uses `<` min_apex break (equivalent, break ok since sorted).

  Wait, actually check: order includes only inten > noise peaks; but pick walks frame peaks [a,b) with no intensity filter. So extension members can be ≤ noise. OpenMS work_exp only contains > noise peaks. Difference changes membership → centroid → which peaks match → which traces exist.

  4. **Tolerance semantics**: OpenMS uses `ftl_sd = (centroid_mz / 1e6) * mass_error_ppm_` and accepts within ±3*ftl_sd — i.e., effective tolerance is 3× mass_error_ppm at the CURRENT centroid, AND with `reestimate_mt_sd=true` (the default!) ftl_sd is dynamically re-estimated via updateWeightedSDEstimateRobust (line 438-441), which can shrink or grow the window far from ppm-based. The tool's detectTraces_ doesn't set reestimate_mt_sd, so default true is active. The integer detector uses a FIXED ppm span per frame from the centroid bin: `ax.span(centroid, b, ppm)` ≈ ppm at centroid — that's 1× ppm, not 3×, and no SD reestimation. Wait — careful: OpenMS `findBestPeak_` searches within ±3*ftl_sd and picks... for IM data, the candidate closest in m/z among those within IM tolerance. Then isPeakAcceptable_ re-checks within ±3*ftl_sd. So yes, OpenMS's acceptance window is ±3 × (ppm × mz/1e6) initially = ±3×20ppm = 60 ppm! Hmm, but with reestimate_mt_sd=true, after first peaks ftl_sd becomes data-driven: `updateWeightedSDEstimateRobust` computes sd from log of deviations... starting from last_weights_sum = apex intensity. First update: denom = sqrt(exp(log(I0)+2log(sd0)) + exp(log(I1)+2log(|mz-mean|)))... The sd generally SHRINKS to reflect actual scatter, typically well below ppm setting — this is why OpenMS docs say mass_error_ppm is initial estimate. So actual OpenMS window is 3× a robust SD estimate, usually much tighter than 3×ppm but possibly wider than 1×ppm early. The integer detector's fixed 1×ppm span is a materially different tolerance. This changes which peaks join → which traces exist. The design's claim "matching by a ppm tolerance on those doubles" understates: it's 3× an iteratively reestimated SD seeded at ppm.

  5. **Candidate selection criterion**: OpenMS IM path (findBestPeak_ lines 324-339): among peaks within ±3sd m/z AND within IM tolerance of the RUNNING IM centroid, pick the one CLOSEST IN M/Z. The integer pick(): closest in tof among unused within span and |scan - scan0| ≤ stol — but scan0 is the SEED's scan, fixed, not the running IM centroid! OpenMS updates centroid_im iteratively (processPeak_ lines 423-426) and uses the running centroid_im in findBestPeak_. The integer version gates IM against the seed's scan with a per-frame tolerance, never updates the IM centroid during extension. A trace whose IM drifts (real 1/K0 drift over RT) would diverge: OpenMS follows the drift; the integer version is pinned to the seed scan. Also the design claims "comparing scans is what the gate actually wants" — that's the downstream gate, but for TRACE EXTENSION OpenMS uses running centroid. This changes which peaks join → trace existence.

  Also OpenMS tie-break: closest m/z; on equal distance keeps the first found (strictly `<`). Integer: `d < best_d` strictly, walking ascending tof — so ties go to the LOWER tof (first encountered below?). Walk starts at lower bound ascending; on ties keeps earlier = lower tof. OpenMS MZBegin order also ascending. Roughly similar.

  6. **Empty spectra**: OpenMS skips empty spectra without incrementing termination counters but DOES increment scan_counter (line 571-572: --trace_down_idx; ++scan_counter happen regardless; only consecutive_missed not incremented for empty spectra). Actually look: if spectrum empty, skip candidate evaluation (no hit, no miss increment), then --trace_down_idx, ++down_state.scan_counter. So empty spectra count in scan_counter for sample_rate denominator but not for outlier counter. In the integer version, pick() returns -1 for empty frame (b<=a) and it counts as a miss (++visited, hits unchanged → rate drops). In outlier mode OpenMS: empty spectrum doesn't increment consecutive_missed, so direction continues indefinitely through empty frames. Integer: empty frames push rate down and break the direction. Different. Though in a window with all frames present, maybe rare. But windows in the compact store: are all frames guaranteed nonempty? Empty frames CAN exist (no peaks above noise). Note OpenMS work_exp contains only spectra with MSLevel 1 — all frames are added even if empty after filtering (tmp_spec could be empty). So this matters.

  7. **Apex re-seeding / max_traces**: not relevant.

  8. **`min_trace_length` check**: OpenMS uses rt_range = |last RT - first RT| of trace — same as integer (rtAxis()[xi.back()] - rtAxis()[xi.front()]). But OpenMS requires `rt_range >= min_trace_length_` (returns false if <) — integer: `if (min_len_sec > 0.0 && len < min_len_sec) continue` — when min_len_sec == 0, OpenMS still rejects rt_range < 0? min_trace_length 5.0 default; tool passes its own. Equivalent when tool sets it. Fine. Also OpenMS checks `max_trace_length_ >= 0.0` — integer `> 0.0` — if max_len == 0, OpenMS rejects any trace with rt_range > 0 (nearly all), integer accepts. Edge: tool default -1, so fine but sloppy.

  9. **Trace validity → visited marking**: already covered (biggest).

  10. **Interleaved bidirectional extension**: OpenMS alternates down/up in ONE while loop (lines 540-648), so centroid updates from the down direction affect the SAME iteration's up extension, and crucially both directions extend against a centroid built from BOTH sides' peaks so far. The integer version does dir=+1 fully to termination, THEN dir=-1 from the seed... wait, re-read: `for (int dir = 1; dir >= -1; dir -= 2)` — direction +1 runs to completion from f0 outward, using evolving centroid (starting from seed), then direction -1 runs from f0-1 outward, but centroid at the start of direction -1 is the FINAL centroid from direction +1 (it persists: centroid, wsum, msum are declared before the dir loop). OpenMS alternates one step each, so each direction sees the combined centroid as it grows symmetrically. Extension order differs → different peak choices in contested cases → different traces. Also OpenMS hits/visited in sample_rate mode are COMBINED across directions (total_hits = down+up+1, total_scans = down+up+1) and checked separately per direction — weird but that's the semantics. Integer computes per-direction hits/visited. Different termination behavior.

  11. **Direction order**: integer does +1 first; OpenMS does down first in each iteration (down = lower RT). Order matters only through centroid state.

  12. **Apex peak marked visited even if trace invalid**: In OpenMS, if the trace is invalid, NONE of its gathered peaks are marked visited — including the apex; the apex will be re-encountered? No — the apex loop `continue`s on visited only; an unvisited apex that produced an invalid trace is NOT re-seeded because the for loop moves on (each apex processed once). But its peaks remain available as EXTENSION candidates for other traces, and other apices among them can seed new traces. In integer: apex marked used immediately; if trace invalid (len check fail), apex and members stay used → can never seed or join. Changes which traces exist.

  13. **`used` in pick() excludes candidate; OpenMS `isPeakAcceptable_` also checks peak_visited. Equivalent in principle, but timing differences above.

  14. **Quantisation of centroid**: integer rounds centroid to nearest bin each step (llround of msum/wsum). OpenMS keeps double centroid. Rounding the running centroid to bin granularity (~0.5 bin error) then computing span from rounded centroid — small but accumulates drift bias; also `pick` uses integer centroid. Minor.

  15. **span uses per-frame b**: integer uses sl.b[f] per frame; OpenMS uses calibrated m/z doubles directly (the materialised map). Both fine; the integer per-frame span conversion in TOF space: tolerance in m/z ppm converts to bins ~ ppm/2 relative... fine as derived. But note: `ax.span(centroid, b[f], ppm)` — OpenMS effective window 3*sd as discussed.

  16. **Noise threshold strictness**: OpenMS keeps peaks with int > noise (strictly); integer order filter `> noise` — same for seeds. Members unfiltered (point 3).

  17. **The seed's own intensity check**: OpenMS apex must be > snr*noise (chrom_apices built from those) AND the apex is only a seed; extension members just need > noise. Integer: break when inten < min_apex — but order was filtered by > noise, and break at first seed < min_apex (sorted desc, so equivalent). OK.

  18. **Trace emission minimum size**: OpenMS has no explicit min size 2 — isTraceValid_ only length & quality. A trace with 1 point has rt_range 0 < min_trace_length → rejected. Equivalent.

  19. **`updateWeightedMeanRT/MZ`** at the end for reporting — integer recomputes; reporting differences only.

  20. **Mobility reporting**: integer reports im = weighted mean over members; OpenMS setCentroidIM(centroid_im) — same iterative weighted mean. Fine.

