The grep for the DIAtracer checkout returned nothing — either the file doesn't exist there or no match. Doesn't matter much; speXtract links "an existing OpenMS installation" — which one? There's /Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS/install. I read the source in /Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS/src — presumably the one. Good enough; I'll note the assumption.

  The `t_mat` timer already exists and is reported separately ("materialize" line). So the split between materialisation and detection is ALREADY measured in every run log! The user says trace detection is 3,869 s (90.9%) — that must be the `t_trace` line, and materialisation has its own line. So for Q3: the design's "no PeakMap materialisation" gain is bounded by the materialize line's own percentage — ask: what does the S30 log say for materialize? I don't have the log, but I can point out the measurement already exists and the design should quote it. Actually the design claims "no double conversion — one pass over 1.2e9 peaks removed" as a time gain, and memory −1..−2 GB. If materialize is separately reported and small (likely a few %), then the claimed gain must come almost entirely from cache behavior during detection — and the falsifier question resolves differently. Also note: with integer_detector, t_mat measures toSlab instead (which does the tofOf conversion per peak — one pass with llround and calibration math per peak, NOT free). Also EPD: t_trace includes do_split (EPD) for the OpenMS path (detectTraces_ includes EPD inside, t2→t3 wraps the whole call). The comment at 1485 says EPD is "the dominant serial phase (EPD-off S30 20:46 vs 28:40 for MS2 alone)" — so EPD is a LARGE share of t_trace! 20:46 vs 28:40 — that's ~28% EPD. Wait, that comment says valley-splitting is the dominant serial phase... "EPD-off S30 20:46 vs 28:40" — total run? For MS2 alone? If t_trace = 3,869 s includes EPD, and EPD is ~28% of it, then the integer detector only addresses the non-EPD part — UNLESS the integer path currently SKIPS EPD entirely (the integer branch at 2962-2993 never calls do_split/EPD!). Look: the integer branch calls detectTracesInteger_ and never runs ElutionPeakDetection. The design says "ElutionPeakDetection runs afterwards on the traces this produces, exactly as it does now" — but the code doesn't do that; there's no EPD adapter for Trace. So as drafted, detector=integer silently drops valley splitting → merged traces → the exact failure mode documented at line 1478-1484 (and ms2_split default 7.0 = ON). This is a top finding for Q4/Q1: the design's "out of scope, unchanged" is currently false; an EPD-on-Trace adapter must be written, and its cost (~28% of t_trace if the 20:46/28:40 numbers are per-phase) remains in BOTH paths, capping the achievable trace-phase reduction: if EPD is ~28% of the 3,869 s and untouched, even a free detector caps the phase reduction at ~72%... i.e. max gain = (3869 - EPD - other)/3869. Let me quantify: if detector proper is D and EPD is E, phase = D + E; a 30-50% cut of the phase requires cutting D by 30-50%×(D+E)/D. If E ≈ 28% of phase, D cut must be 41-69%. Hmm, but the 20:46 vs 28:40 was wall time of the whole run for an EPD-off A/B, old run. The 3,869 s figure is from the current phase table. I can't decompose it without the log. Point stands: EPD share of t_trace must be subtracted before estimating, and the integer path must keep EPD.

